Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);

    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        // Convert each line to HTML
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Title\n\nThis is a first paragraph.\n\nThis is a second paragraph.\n\nThis is a third paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and converts each line to HTML by wrapping it in `<p>` tags. The resulting HTML string is then returned.

The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function and prints the resulting HTML text.

